P-I5, spec 001 group 8 (W1): the edge's protected Terraform and the probe's switch, for the Author's hand.

Built from plain copies: infra/account/alerts.tf as P-I3 leaves it (a git archive export of 7c5f1259 with W1's eight
patches applied in order; P-I7 and P-I4 don't touch it), and /dev/null for the two new files; edited in $TMPDIR, then
diff -u with a/ and b/ labels after this preamble; never git apply, and nothing written into any worktree. Apply it on
W1's -p branch after P-I4 (and so after P-I3), with `patch -p1 < <this file>` at the repository's root, and commit the
result byte for byte (tasks.md T107, T114, T171, T172).

What it holds (tasks.md T104, T107; contracts/edge.md; contracts/aws-alerts.md §5; plan.md §16, "Why a switch",
"Group 8, as built"):
  - infra/prod/edge.tf, new, under spec 000's protected names (infra/*edge*): the site's origin access control and
    the CloudFront Function that serves a folder's index.html, from W1; and behind prod's local.wired, W2's: the
    certificate for hareet.ai and www.hareet.ai, validated by DNS; the
    VPC origin to the relay on the tunnel end, 8080; and the distribution, with the origins `site` and `relay`,
    /api/* uncached to the relay, 502 and 504 as 503 with /_degraded/api.json for 0 s, and no access log;
  - infra/prod/edge-limits.tf, new, rule T5: CloudFront's cache policy for the site, its managed CachingDisabled read
    for /api/*, the relay's origin-request policy (CloudFront-Viewer-Address, Content-Type, Accept, traceparent; no
    cookie, no query string), and the site's response headers (spec 002's CSP, HSTS, no-referrer, nosniff, and the
    Permissions-Policy). W1 makes them; W2's distribution uses them;
  - infra/account/alerts.tf: the probe's schedule enabled by the account's local.wired, and its alarm,
    h4-probe-failing, behind it: 5 failed minutes, missing data breaching, to h4-alerts on alarm and on recovery.
    W1 holds the switch off, so P-I5 enables nothing in W1; P-W2 enables both in W2, after prod's apply.

Its checks, on a fresh export with P-I3, P-G1, P-G3, P-I7, P-G4, P-G2, P-I4, P-G5, P-6, this patch, and P-G6, in that
order: infra/test (test_prod_cdn.py among them) passes with each switch off and on; `make infra-check`'s policy
finds nothing but prod's lockfile (T10, the Author's step since group 5); terraform fmt is clean.
